"A scatter-gather diagram distributes one request to several recipients and combines correlated responses under a completion policy.\n\nUse when: Review parallel queries or multi-provider aggregation. Explain timeout, quorum, and partial-result behavior.\n\nAvoid when: Messages are broadcast without an aggregated response.\n\nVisual grammar: A scatter node fans one request into recipient paths. A gather node collects correlated responses. Completion annotations specify required recipients, deadlines, or partial-result rules.\n\nBuild: Define the request identity and the intended recipient set. Draw parallel recipient paths between scatter and gather. State completion and missing-response policies explicitly. Align recipients in separate lanes and route all responses into clear gather ports.\n\nCheck: Gathering cannot wait forever without a documented policy. Duplicate responses do not count as additional recipients. Partial results are distinguished from complete results. Fan-out and fan-in connectors remain separate from recipient labels.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming every recipient responds exactly once. Drawing a join that silently requires all responses despite a partial-result contract.\n\nExample: A travel search queries three carriers and returns correlated offers after all replies or its stated deadline.\n\nAlternatives: Request-reply diagram, Activity diagram."
